Title: "Predicates and Quantifiers"
Id: "70fb4c9d-8dde-4109-a032-6da3247867c1"


Text: "While propositional logic provides the rules for combining complete,
       self-contained assertions, it is blind to the internal anatomy of
       statements. In propositional logic, the statements 'Every continuous
       function on a closed interval is bounded' and '$7$ is a prime number' are
       merely opaque atomic variables $P$ and $Q$. Propositional logic cannot
       express the fact that $7$ possesses a property shared by other numbers,
       nor can it account for the word 'every'.

       To capture the full depth of mathematical reasoning, we must peer inside
       assertions. In 1879, Gottlob Frege revolutionized logic by replacing the
       traditional subject-predicate grammar of classical philosophy with the
       mathematical concept of a *function*. An assertion is understood as a
       property or relation—a *predicate*—applied to one or more mathematical
       objects. By introducing *quantifiers* that govern variables ranging over
       specified domains, we obtain *first-order predicate logic*, the
       expressive medium in which modern mathematics is written."
Id: "3ea75cf2-0c9a-4882-ad3f-ed0c34a3a53c"


SectionTitle: "Predicates, Domains, and Variables"
Id: "76392982-4763-4264-aea1-c7bac3d909fa"


Text: "A *predicate* is an incomplete statement containing one or more open
       variables. On its own, an open expression such as '$x^2 < 2$' or '$x$
       divides $y$' is neither true nor false; it becomes a definite proposition
       only when specific objects are substituted for its variables, or when
       those variables are bound by quantifiers.

       Crucially, every variable in mathematics belongs to an explicit or
       implicit *universe of discourse* or *domain*. The statement '$x^2 = 2$'
       has solutions if the domain of $x$ is the real numbers $\mathbb{R}$, but
       has no solutions if the domain is restricted to the rational numbers
       $\mathbb{Q}$. Specifying the domain is not an incidental detail; it is an
       organic part of the predicate's definition."
Id: "2498f869-7009-411a-bc01-9a76ffa07912"


TextDefinition: "Let $D_1,\ldots,D_n$ be domains. An $n$-ary predicate $P$ on
                 these domains is an expression for which $P(a_1,\ldots,a_n)$ is
                 a statement whenever $a_i\in D_i$ for every $i$. Equivalently,
                 after a semantics has been fixed, $P$ determines a truth-valued
                 function on $D_1\times\cdots\times D_n$. The domains are part
                 of the specification: the same formula may define different
                 predicates when its variables range over different objects."
Id: "5425d771-9398-4949-aeac-86991b073f13"


Text: "In any formula involving variables, we must scrupulously distinguish
       between *free* variables and *bound* variables. A free variable acts as
       an open slot or parameter: it can be assigned different values from its
       domain, and the truth of the formula varies with the assignment. A bound
       variable, by contrast, is governed by a quantifier ($\forall$ or
       $\exists$); it is an internal dummy variable whose scope is restricted to
       the quantified expression."
Id: "7820a647-38ae-4f70-b184-a134d193d502"


TextDefinition: "An occurrence of a variable is *bound* if it lies within the
                 scope of a quantifier that binds that variable; otherwise it is
                 *free*. A formula with no free variables is a sentence and
                 therefore has a truth value in a fixed interpretation. A
                 formula with free variables denotes a condition on possible
                 assignments to those variables. Renaming a bound variable does
                 not change meaning, provided the new name does not capture a
                 formerly free occurrence."
Id: "4d77ab37-a8da-4d6b-b9c7-d5dc9036755d"


Text: "This distinction between free and bound variables is entirely analogous
       to familiar constructs in calculus and linear algebra: the index $i$ in a
       sum $\sum_{i=1}^n a_i$ or the variable $t$ in a definite integral
       $\int_a^b f(t)\,dt$ is bound, while $n$, $a$, and $b$ are free. Renaming
       $t$ to $u$ in $\int_a^b f(u)\,du$ leaves the integral unchanged (a
       property known in logic as *$\alpha$-equivalence*).

       Care is required when substituting expressions into formulas: if a
       substituted term contains a variable that happens to match a quantifier
       inside the formula, accidental *variable capture* can occur, radically
       corrupting the meaning. A rigorous deductive system systematically
       renames bound variables whenever substitution threatens to capture them."
Id: "4289625b-21c1-495f-889f-ea1d4d707fd6"


SectionTitle: "Universal and Existential Quantification"
Id: "dc40bcba-5c18-4126-86c9-56e178a65d78"


Text: "Quantifiers turn open predicates into closed propositions by asserting
       how widely a condition holds across its domain. Predicate logic
       introduces two fundamental quantifiers: the universal quantifier
       ($\forall$) and the existential quantifier ($\exists$)."
Id: "64738a4d-451e-9a18-971c-26a578912301"


TextDefinition: "For a predicate $P(x)$ on a domain $D$, the universal statement
                 $\forall x\in D\,P(x)$ (read *for all $x$ in $D$, $P(x)$*) is
                 true exactly when $P(a)$ is true for every single element
                 $a\in D$.

                 To prove a universal assertion, one must consider an
                 *arbitrary* element $x \in D$ and prove that $P(x)$ holds using
                 only the defining properties of $D$, without assuming any
                 special traits of the chosen element. Conversely, to refute a
                 universal assertion, one need only exhibit a single
                 *counterexample*—an element $c \in D$ for which $P(c)$ fails."
Id: "549d47c5-700f-4cd5-86a2-26533b189178"


TextDefinition: "For a predicate $P(x)$ on a domain $D$, the existential
                 statement $\exists x\in D\,P(x)$ (read *there exists $x$ in $D$
                 such that $P(x)$*) is true exactly when at least one element
                 $a\in D$ satisfies $P(a)$. Such an element is called a
                 *witness*.

                 An existential claim can be proved *constructively* by
                 explicitly producing a witness and verifying that it satisfies
                 $P$, or *non-constructively* by demonstrating that the
                 assumption that no witness exists leads to an insurmountable
                 contradiction. Note that an existential assertion guarantees
                 only that *at least one* witness exists; it makes no claim
                 regarding whether the witness is unique."
Id: "60a3796d-d17b-4e3f-b2a1-1878e1468e61"


Text: "Just as De Morgan's laws govern the interaction between negation,
       conjunction, and disjunction in propositional logic, an identical duality
       governs the negation of quantifiers. Negating a universal claim produces
       an existential claim, and negating an existential claim produces a
       universal claim:
       $$\neg \forall x \in D \, P(x) \iff \exists x \in D \, \neg P(x)$$
       $$\neg \exists x \in D \, P(x) \iff \forall x \in D \, \neg P(x)$$

       To deny that every swan is white is not to claim that every swan is
       black; it is to assert the existence of at least one non-white swan.
       Dually, to deny that a system of equations has a solution is to assert
       that every proposed candidate fails to solve it."
Id: "3748291a-bc01-411a-7009-9a76ffa07999"


Theorem:
given:
. D is \set
. P(x_) is (_ is \\anything) -> (_ is \\statement)
then:
. not:
  . forAll: x "in" D
    then: P(x)
iff:
. exists: x "in" D
  suchThat:
  . not: P(x)
Documented:
. called: "Negation of a Universal Quantifier"
. description: "A universal assertion fails exactly when there is a
                counterexample in its domain."
Proof: """By definition, the statement $\forall x \in D, P(x)$ is true if and
          only if $P(x)$ holds for every element $x \in D$. Its negation asserts
          that it is false that every element satisfies $P(x)$. This occurs
          precisely when there is at least one element $x \in D$ for which
          $P(x)$ fails to hold, which is the definition of
          $\exists x \in D, \neg P(x)$."""
Id: "bf4df541-b2ff-4fff-9f32-e0565e88a24a"


Theorem:
given:
. D is \set
. P(x_) is (_ is \\anything) -> (_ is \\statement)
then:
. not:
  . exists: x "in" D
    suchThat: P(x)
iff:
. forAll: x "in" D
  then:
  . not: P(x)
Documented:
. called: "Negation of an Existential Quantifier"
. description: "An existential assertion fails exactly when every element of its
                domain fails the predicate."
Proof: """By definition, the statement $\exists x \in D, P(x)$ is true if and
          only if there exists at least one witness $x \in D$ satisfying $P(x)$.
          The negation asserts that no such witness exists. In other words,
          every candidate $x \in D$ fails to satisfy $P(x)$, which is precisely
          the assertion $\forall x \in D, \neg P(x)$."""
Id: "4396c679-4735-45fb-ba6a-704ee55fc63b"


Text: "When multiple quantifiers appear in a single formula, their ordering is
       of paramount mathematical consequence. In predicate calculus, *quantifier
       order records dependency*.

       Consider a binary relation $R(x, y)$ over a domain $D$. The statement:
       $$\forall x \, \exists y \, R(x, y)$$ asserts that for each individual
       $x$, there exists a corresponding $y$. Crucially, the choice of $y$ is
       permitted to depend directly upon $x$. By contrast, the statement:
       $$\exists y \, \forall x \, R(x, y)$$ demands that a single, universal
       witness $y$ must be chosen *in advance*, functioning simultaneously for
       all possible values of $x$.

       In colloquial terms, 'Everyone has a mother'
       ($\forall x \, \exists y \, M(y, x)$) is an ordinary biological truth;
       but 'There is someone who is the mother of everyone'
       ($\exists y \, \forall x \, M(y, x)$) is an absurdity.

       In higher mathematics, this distinction between $\forall x \exists y$ and
       $\exists y \forall x$ separates elementary properties from deep
       structural ones. In real analysis, it is precisely the distinction
       between *pointwise continuity* (where for every point $x$ and tolerance
       $\varepsilon$ there exists a radius $\delta(x, \varepsilon)$) and
       *uniform continuity* (where a single radius $\delta(\varepsilon)$ must
       work uniformly across the entire domain)."
Id: "f983ba30-e2c8-45f9-afd9-8e19e841e502"


SectionTitle: "Existence and Uniqueness"
Id: "cdaab479-a146-47b9-b147-de10ed82aa04"


Text: "Mathematics frequently encounters situations where an object satisfying a
       given condition is not merely present, but unique. We denote this by the
       quantifier $\exists!$, read *there exists a unique*."
Id: "4810a647-38ae-4f70-b184-a134d193d503"


TextDefinition: "The notation $\exists!x\in D\,P(x)$ asserts existence and
                 uniqueness: there is an $a\in D$ satisfying $P(a)$, and any
                 $b\in D$ satisfying $P(b)$ equals $a$. A proof therefore has
                 two logically independent parts. It is often useful to prove
                 uniqueness conditionally before constructing an object, because
                 the uniqueness argument identifies which properties a
                 successful construction must have."
Id: "730615eb-feb0-45d1-8ca7-82c3138f5eb7"


Text: "The quantifier $\exists!$ is the logical license for introducing
       *definite descriptions* and functional notations. In his landmark 1905
       theory of descriptions, Bertrand Russell emphasized that an expression
       such as 'the identity element of $G$' or 'the limit of $f(x)$ as
       $x \to a$' is meaningless unless both existence and uniqueness have been
       established."
Id: "5912a647-38ae-4f70-b184-a134d193d504"


TextTheorem: "If $\exists!x\in D\,P(x)$, then a definition of the form 'let $a$
              be the unique element of $D$ satisfying $P$' is unambiguous.
              Without prior existence and uniqueness, the same phrase does not
              define an object: it merely presupposes one."
Id: "09f10995-86d7-409a-8411-247d4acef1f4"


Text: "Finally, we examine the formal structure of *restricted quantifiers*. When
       we write $\forall x \in D \, P(x)$ or $\exists x \in D \, P(x)$, the domain
       restriction is syntactic sugar whose underlying expansion uses the standard
       Boolean connectives:
       $$\forall x \in D \, P(x) \quad \equiv \quad \forall x \, (x \in D \Rightarrow P(x))$$
       $$\exists x \in D \, P(x) \quad \equiv \quad \exists x \, (x \in D \land P(x))$$

       The change from conditional ($\Rightarrow$) in the universal quantifier to
       conjunction ($\land$) in the existential quantifier is of the utmost logical
       importance. If one mistakenly wrote $\exists x \, (x \in D \Rightarrow P(x))$,
       the assertion would become trivially true in any universe containing at least
       one object outside $D$! For any element $c \notin D$, the antecedent $c \in D$
       is false, making the conditional $c \in D \Rightarrow P(c)$ vacuously true,
       thereby providing a false witness for the existential claim. Conjunction
       ensures that an existential witness must legitimately reside within the
       intended domain $D$."
Id: "64c6c4ea-6a77-413f-9dda-b1d4bb102443"
